    "knowledge_point": "Finding the First Few Terms of a Binomial Expansion",
    "problem": "$(3+x)^{4}=$",
    "worked_solution": "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{0}){a}^{n}{b}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{1}){a}^{n-1}{b}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+⋯+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of $(3+x)^{4},$ we have  $a=3,$ $b=x,$ $n=4.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of $(3+x)^{4}$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3+x)}^{4} & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{0}){(3)}^{4}{(x)}^{0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{1}){(3)}^{4-1}{(x)}^{1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2}){(3)}^{4-2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1{(3)}^{4}+4{(3)}^{3}{(x)}^{1}+6{(3)}^{2}{(x)}^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =81+108x+54{x}^{2}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
